Abstraction is no refuge from AI
Reliable computation and the test of specialist advantage
Henry Shin | 4 November 2026 | Revised 18 November 2026
Text revision 7e8b863064f9

AI already invents representations, finds proofs, and recognizes when a search has exhausted its own assumptions. Where does it still pay to put a mathematician beside the machine? I would look where everything measured so far agrees and the distinction that matters is still invisible. Someone has to find a way to see it and prove that it changes the answer. Adaptive sparse computation is where I would test that wager first.

1. What the evidence changes

The old refuge in abstraction is over. Anyone moving deeper into algebraic geometry, representation theory, abstract algebra, or theoretical PDE for shelter from AI is counting on a barrier AI has already crossed. The published work reaches the conceptual acts that were supposed to confer protection. Machines have supplied a missing dimension argument, devised a proof strategy that collaborators carried out, and connected theories that arose in different subjects. Mathematical depth still matters. As a reason to expect the machine to stop, it has failed. [1, 2, 3, 8, 16]

Four vertices are enough to expose the difficulty. Take a cycle with alternating strong and weak edges. A solver groups the vertices joined by each strong edge so it can correct errors shared by the pair. Now exchange the weights while keeping the old pairs. The matrix has the same size, the same pattern of nonzeros, the same diagonal, even the same eigenvalues. Give both vertices of one new strong pair error +1 and both vertices of the other error −1. The residual sums to zero on each old pair. From those two sums alone, the coarse solver sees exactly what it would see if the answer were already right.

Those two sums have lost the relation between the strong edges and the old pairs. In the Deligne-Drinfeld proof, a projection throws away an entire variable, yet the defining relations recover what the theorem needs. Four vertices on a cycle and polynomials in a Lie algebra pose the same question. Which loss matters? [1]

Someone has to find an observation that exposes the error, coordinates that make a proof possible, or an obstruction that rules out a whole search. Agents have done all three. A case for hiring a specialist has to say what happens when the machine gets a fair shot at finding the decisive move. [1, 70, 105, 106]

My first bet is adaptive sparse computation. A good solver already exists. Change its coefficients, its arithmetic, its hardware or the number of solves left to run, and the decision to retain, repair or rebuild it opens again. A specialist who recognizes the failure early can save an expensive search. An agent gets to learn from the same independent error checks. Repeated solves can repay a good diagnosis and teach the machine to make the next one for itself. [39, 48, 62]

I would fund that work even if agents made all the decisive discoveries. My stronger bet is that, at a fixed research budget, adding a specialist raises the chance of an accepted discovery more on the selected adaptive-sparse projects than on complete structural-mathematics projects. A single abstract proof can kill the promise of shelter. Ranking fields takes a comparison. Give rival teams every successful idea, charge for failed searches and human hours, and see who finds the next one. [90, 101, 106]

That comparison takes trained mathematicians as it finds them. What it costs to produce people who can still diagnose a failure independently, and who bears that cost, deserve a separate account. [What Sustains Mathematical Expertise](https://refactor.press/essays/what-sustains-mathematical-expertise#sustaining-independent-capability)


2. Conceptual work is already being automated

A proof can turn on deciding what to forget. The Deligne-Drinfeld conjecture identifies the rational Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra with the free graded Lie algebra on one generator in each odd degree starting at three. A rational lower bound was known. Thomas Willwacher credits OpenAI with the missing upper bound and gives a shorter, AI-assisted proof. The equations have integer coefficients. Passing to characteristic two can enlarge their solution space, so an upper bound there also bounds its rational dimension. [1]

In characteristic two write A = x², B = y and C = [x,y]. Now set A to zero. At weight three the projection does this. [1]

P₃ = [A,B] + [B,C],    P₃(0,B,C) = [B,C].

The first term has disappeared. Yet a representation of the four-strand braid Lie algebra turns the defining equations into a formula that recovers the whole polynomial from its projection. On the constrained space the projection is injective, and its image lies in Lie(C,[B,C]). Counting dimensions in that smaller space gives the required bound in weights at least three. Willwacher credits Leila Schneps and Benjamin Enriquez with a reworking that inspired his simplified formula for the representation. Nikita Markarian subsequently explained the projection geometrically through the double cover that sends z to z² and the filling of a puncture. One can recognize what the model contributed and still see what later mathematicians invented. [1, 2]

Aletheia found another use for a change of representation. Tony Feng credits the agent with connecting arithmetic eigenweights to symmetric-group representation theory through algebraic combinatorics and determining them for all classical groups. In type D of even rank, a commutativity assumption needed for an earlier result can fail. Two natural generators have to be treated through their two-dimensional span. Apart from building the agent, Feng describes his own role as rewriting its mathematics into a paper. The same team records a different division of work on independence polynomials, where Aletheia suggested the roadmap and the human collaborators supplied the proofs. [3]

Blind review offers a different check. In First Proof’s ten-problem third batch, GPT-6 Astra earned credit on seven problems and ProofCouncil on nine. On the Darcy inverse problem all three reviewers judged ProofCouncil’s mathematics essentially correct. It removed a logarithmic loss in the human upper bound and earned full marks for correctness and exposition, zero for attribution. In TheoPhysEval, some apparent Astra errors sent the experts back to their own reference solutions. They found mistakes there and corrected them. The Darcy reviewers wanted Richter’s inversion formula credited where the proof used it. Contributing a better bound and explaining where its ingredients came from are two jobs, and the reviewers could tell which one the system had done. [8, 9]

A mathematician looking for a field where this cannot happen gets little help from a leaderboard. OpenAI’s larger catalogue describes work at different stages of verification. The census in [Appendix A](#mathematical-and-benchmark-record) records results in seventeen subjects and gives no attempted-problem counts by field. Its coverage tells us where to look for more examples. It cannot tell a mathematician how the odds change on moving from one field to another. [4]

Return to the four-cycle. Its complete spectrum survives the exchange of weights. The old pairs miss the same error before and after the exchange. What changes is that relaxation used to shrink it and now scarcely touches it. In the Lie algebra a visibly nonzero term disappears, yet the equations force its recovery. Counting what survives gives the wrong intuition in both cases. The solver needs to know where its pairs lie relative to the strong edges. The theorem needs only what its relations can reconstruct. Choosing a representation means knowing which distinction the next mathematical step will need. [1]


3. A contribution has to earn its value

Research taste has to earn its keep. If an agent can choose a representation, what choice would I pay a mathematician to make? Yisong Yue, discussing the Caltech-AIM platform, locates taste in human experience even as he assigns the machine a role in proposing conjectures, connections and strategies. To assess such a division of work, follow one choice far enough to see what it changes. Did it expose a false assumption, lead to a theorem or spare an expensive search? What did it cost to make and check it? [91]

Catching a missing hypothesis can decide whether a theorem stands. Wang and collaborators asked seven general-purpose model snapshots to translate mathematical statements into a more abstract vocabulary. On their held-out benchmark, 60.6 percent of those rewrites widened the asserted domain. A faithful translation has to carry the restriction across, and a genuine generalization needs a proof. That gives a specific failure to look for in newer systems. It leaves open who will catch it most economically. [10]

Inventing the subtle algorithm is open to the same comparison. Alman and Vassilevska Williams credit Claude with an algorithm they then understood, simplified, strengthened and extended. For polynomially bounded integer inputs their bounds include O(n^1.9992) for 3SUM and O(n^2.9995) for directed all-pairs shortest paths with no negative cycles. The contribution is mathematical even though those exponents give no practical crossover. In operator theory Townsend and Greenbaum report that they and Crouzeix checked Jin’s AI-assisted proof of Crouzeix’s conjecture and believe it correct. Its decisive theorem emerged in an approximately sixteen-hour model run. Designing a numerical method that can use the theorem economically is a further research question, open to either partner. [17, 18]

SciPiVot gets closer to measuring judgment. Its authors reconstructed 248 pivotal decisions from 26 scientific projects. PhD students and postdocs new to the projects, with an AI assistant available, chose the better-supported next step in 73.4 percent of cases. The best tested model with the project record scored 63.5 percent. Both plans were supplied, and the cases had been selected retrospectively from human corrections. Recognizing the better plan once someone has written it is one skill. Inventing it at the point of failure and paying for the unsuccessful alternatives is the harder comparison. [61]

The structural mathematician must get the whole job in that comparison. Let the team choose its definitions, find an obstruction and decide what would constitute an explanation. Likewise, let the solver team discover that its original performance target misses the error it cares about. Both teams may begin with the same question. What can a cheaper representation discard while still carrying the claim?


4. Why compression still matters

Michael Atiyah offered a historical reason for abstraction. Humans were poor at large calculations, so much of theory developed as a way around them. He wondered whether machines good at calculation would feel the same pressure to find conceptual alternatives. Consider what happens after a mathematician finds one. Wilf-Zeilberger theory gives certificates for suitable hypergeometric identities. A computer can search for a certificate, verify its identity and check the boundary conditions. A good abstraction makes a whole class of later problems cheaper, including for a machine. [81, 82]

Machines encounter those incentives during discovery too. FunSearch found constructor programs whose symmetries helped its authors understand the objects they generated. AlphaEvolve can search over objects, programs that build them or algorithms that perform the search. Each choice changes how much work it takes to find the next example. In my work on [Taiko product structures](https://refactor.press/papers/taiko-girth-obstruction), translating a group-ring construction into incidence geometry exposed pressure and intersection constraints that the coarse counts concealed. A representation can make a computation cheaper, make a distinction visible, or do both. [83, 44, 80]

Two model-produced graph manuscripts already combine compression with a bill for recovering what survives. The strong-thin-tree construction controls cut loads while retaining the rank information needed to extract a spanning tree. Its signing routine requires rational vectors with covariance exactly equal to the identity. The proof approximates the frame rationally, adds vectors that exactly complete the covariance, checks positive semidefiniteness and transfers the estimate back after removing the added vectors. Its claimed C/k cut bound has polynomial bit complexity. With the displayed constant 2²⁰ and error 2²⁰ r^(−1/8), the coefficient in the next packing bound turns positive only for r > 2¹⁶⁸. Even positivity need not retain one whole tree. The proof absorbs smaller cases into an absolute constant. The calculation shows how conservative its sufficient guarantee is. A practical crossover is a separate question, as is two-sided spectral approximation. [97]

The matching construction changes sparse forests without losing the physical value of an edge. When it deletes a copy it transfers that copy’s accumulated increment to a base value. It records motion before cancellation so it can detect stale values and pay for refreshing them. A progress account pays for exploring local projections, while exact dyadic updates carry the precision requirement. Its claimed almost-linear randomized word-RAM bound charges allocation, multiword arithmetic, unsuccessful paths, scheduling and output. No competitive finite size follows from the stated asymptotic bound. But any experiment on who can invent a diagnostic must give the autonomous team access to algorithms that already detect and pay for repairs. [98]

Who finds the missing distinction?

Nguyen’s cosmology case records a human intervention. Across 33 sessions an agent varied coefficients, angular terms and quadrature within an analytic projection. Errors remained 8-86 percent. A physicist recognized that the projection had lost the angular dependence of baryon-acoustic-oscillation damping and proposed Gauss-Legendre quadrature for the projection itself. The agent implemented the redesign in one session. Four of the six affected outputs came within one percent of their references. The other two initially differed by about 1-2 percent. Later a fitted scalar passed the tests, but the physicist asked what term belonged inside the integral. Restoring its angular dependence removed the fitted parameter. The supervisor reconstructed parts of this history from memory, and no matched autonomous continuation priced the alternative. [101]

On a sign-uncertainty problem the agents found the turn themselves. Their evaluator accepted Laguerre polynomials with at most twenty prescribed positive double roots. An exact obstruction on 41 tail points showed that no construction in that family could bring the target below 0.315305. The agents retained the Laguerre basis, abandoned the prescribed roots and certified 0.3089 with a polynomial the evaluator could not score. In the cosmology problem, repeated adjustments concealed the need to change a projection. Here, failure became a theorem about the search itself. The mathematical move belongs to whoever can make it. [106]

Even recognizing an obstruction and exploiting it can be two different contributions. After six unsuccessful variations on a rounding scheme for the Grothendieck constant, the operators asked the agent to prove a ceiling for the whole class, a possibility already suggested in the brief. The agent identified the Naor-Regev theorem that could convert a sufficiently general ceiling into a lower bound on the constant. The eventual proof took more work from both. That record tells us who intervened and when. A second continuation from the same state would help tell us what the intervention bought. [105]

I would test this first in adaptive sparse computation because execution keeps reopening decisions. In CAST, extra sampling improves a local bound yet can create fill at later eliminations. It stays cheap on some low-degree graphs, propagates expensively on Chimera systems and behaves differently inside already dense blocks. Pivot degree alone cannot price it. In coarse-space selection the sharper diagnostic can require solves with the original operator. A probe that repays its cost across a thousand solves can consume the whole saving when four remain. Those are concrete reasons to look for discoveries here. Each is also a reason for an agent to learn the rule once somebody finds it. [48, 62, 86]

Structural mathematics has the same pressures. Gröbner-basis algorithms use signatures and syzygies to discard work. Roune and Stillman cut the potentially quadratic number of stored S-pair monomials to linear while retaining an integer per queued pair. In the Liquid Tensor Experiment, understanding what formalization actually required let Commelin and Scholze replace stable-homotopy machinery with a weaker sufficient result. A numerical diagnosis and an algebraic lemma alike can save someone else a search. My wager needs three things. A costly decision must keep returning. The specialist must find the decisive information more economically than the equipped agent. And the advantage must survive handing yesterday’s discovery to everyone. Changing operators, arithmetic and workloads give me a reason to bet on the first. The other two have to be won on fresh problems. [20, 48, 62, 95] [93, 94]

The four-cycle lets us state the numerical obligation exactly. On a small graph, write down the energy a sparse representation must preserve. Then change the weights and follow the error the old representation can no longer see. [19, 58]


5. What a sparse representation must preserve

Start with a finite weighted graph. Orient its edges, let B be the edge-vertex incidence matrix with -1 at each edge's tail and +1 at its head, and put its positive edge weights in the diagonal matrix W. The graph Laplacian L has the following quadratic form.

L = B^T W B,     x^T L x = sum_{edges {i,j}} w_ij (x_i - x_j)^2.

A constant vector has zero energy. For a connected graph this is the entire kernel. One can fix a vertex potential or work on the mean-zero subspace to obtain a positive-definite problem. A related class consists of symmetric diagonally dominant matrices with nonpositive off-diagonal entries, abbreviated SDDM here. Such a matrix has the form A = L + Gamma, where Gamma is nonnegative diagonal. It is positive definite when every connected component has a positive grounding term. The scope here is this concrete matrix class. [19, 26]

Elimination makes the central difficulty visible. Suppose a vertex is joined to three neighbors by edges of weight one. For fixed neighbor values, minimize the energy over the central value. The minimizer is their average. Substitution leaves exactly the energy of a triangle on the three neighbors, each new edge having weight one third. For a star with positive incident-weight vector a, elimination gives the Schur complement below.

S = diag(a) - a a^T / s,     s = sum_i a_i;     a = (1,1,1)^T gives triangle weights 1/3.

Figure: Eliminating the center of a three-edge unit star produces a triangle with edge weights 1/3. For any fixed values at the three remaining vertices, the triangle has exactly the star's minimum energy over the eliminated central value. Both retain the constant-vector nullspace.

Elimination preserves minimized energy exactly. Repeated elimination also couples previously unconnected variables, creating fill. Retaining every new interaction can destroy the economy of a sparse input. Discarding interactions changes the energy that made the elimination correct. Spectral sparsification seeks a much smaller graph whose energy approximates the original for every vector. Effective-resistance sampling assigns importance using the surrounding network as well as the edge weight. [19]

A sharp sparsification theorem shows how quantitative this compression can be. Batson, Spielman, and Srivastava prove that, for 0 < epsilon < 1, a weighted graph on n vertices has a reweighted subgraph with at most ceil((n - 1)/ε²) edges whose energy lies between (1 - epsilon)² and (1 + epsilon)² times the original energy for every vector. The guarantee controls an entire family of calculations. Obtaining such a representation cheaply and using it in an efficient solver are additional algorithmic requirements. [84]

A preconditioned iteration repeatedly applies an inexpensive approximation to the inverse, aiming to reduce the total work needed to solve the original system. Write that action as solving with an auxiliary matrix M. For symmetric positive-definite A and M, suppose positive constants alpha and beta satisfy the following inequalities for every vector x.

alpha x^T M x <= x^T A x <= beta x^T M x.

Then the generalized eigenvalues of A relative to M lie between alpha and beta, and the corresponding condition number is at most beta/alpha. For a fixed positive-definite preconditioner in exact arithmetic, this ratio controls the standard worst-case convergence bound for conjugate gradients. A smaller condition number alone need not produce fewer iterations on a particular problem, since the spectral distribution matters. The computational benefit depends on convergence together with the cost of constructing and applying M. For Laplacians the comparison is made on the orthogonal complement of their common kernel. [52, 75]

Multigrid approaches the loss of information in another way. A cheap relaxation reduces some error components, and a coarse correction represents those that remain. Algebraically smooth error is error that the chosen relaxation struggles to remove. Its meaning depends on the operator and relaxation. Strongly connected regions joined by weak edges can move almost independently at low energy. A coarse representation must account for the resulting modes. The relevant objects are vectors, matrix blocks, graph partitions, and transfer operators. [20]

Accuracy has a similar trap. In the following exact two-variable system, the second variable is weakly weighted.

A = diag(1, 10^-8),  b = (1, 10^-8)^T,  x = (1,1)^T,  y = (1,0)^T.

The relative residual in the infinity norm is 10^-8, while the relative forward error in the same norm is 1. The proposed answer loses the entire second component. Interpreting a residual therefore requires conditioning information and a specified error measure. LAPACK's error analysis makes that distinction explicit. [21]

A more discriminating inexpensive observation exposes this particular failure. With r = b - A y and absolute values taken entrywise, the componentwise backward error is

omega_c = max_i |r_i| / (|A| |y| + |b|)_i = 1.

Its denominator is (2, 10^-8), so the lost second component remains visible. Computing this quantity needs a sparse matrix-vector product, without an inverse. Forward-error control still requires the appropriate conditioning information. The example shows why choosing the right observable can be more useful than refining an aggregate residual. [21]

Refine the mesh in the [Poisson calculation](https://refactor.press/essays/continuity-as-organized-discrete-data#making-the-error-affordable-to-measure) and the zero function earns a smaller absolute Euclidean residual against the nodal test functions. Its relative residual stays at one. Its energy error against the continuum solution never improves. An agent rewarded for shrinking the absolute residual could beat its previous score every time without changing its answer. The missing ingredient is a bound connecting that particular measurement to the error we care about.

A spectral comparison holding for every vector can support every compatible right-hand side for the same operator. Its construction and justification can therefore serve many solves. The research problem is to obtain this control with a representation cheap enough to construct and apply, or to find affordable observations that justify retaining or repairing it after the operator changes. Approximate elimination and coarse spaces expose different versions of this problem. [20, 52, 58]


6. Constructing, diagnosing, and repairing

Eliminating the center of a star preserved its energy at the price of creating a clique. Two ways of avoiding that expense lead to different questions. If we sample the new edges, how far can the energy drift over many eliminations? If we group variables, which errors must survive on the coarse level because relaxation barely touches them? [20, 26, 47]

What one elimination leaves for the next

Gao, Kyng, and Spielman’s AC(2) samples interactions among the neighbors of an eliminated vertex and keeps the sample connected. It performs well on many symmetric diagonally dominant systems with nonpositive off-diagonal entries. The authors conjecture a relative condition number of O(log^2 m), possibly O(log m), with high probability for m input edges. The missing argument must control the random distortions as the Schur complement itself changes. [26]

A triangle shows why connectedness and the right average are insufficient. Start with unit weights. Choose one edge uniformly, give it weight 3 − 2ε, and give the other two weight ε, with 0 < ε < 1. Every outcome is connected and every edge still has expected weight one.

E[L_s] = L,     but kappa(L_s relative to L on the mean-zero subspace) = (2 - epsilon)/epsilon.

If edge 12 is heavy, (1,1,−2) and (1,−1,0) span the mean-zero subspace and have relative eigenvalues ε and 2 − ε. Every realization can therefore be a poor approximation although the average Laplacian is exact. This calculation isolates the gap between those two properties and a spectral guarantee. Actual elimination rules such as VAC and CAST use additional structure. [47, 48]

CAST lets us follow one such choice into the calculation. Increasing its sampling parameter tightens a bound on the local second moment. It also introduces edges that later eliminations may have to process. At the base setting, CAST-1 retained AC’s ordering, factor representation, and solver path. Over 250 right-hand sides its mean per-system speedup over AC was 1.17 on 17 SuiteSparse matrices and 1.18 on 128 Chimera systems. [48]

On one Chimera instance, the better choice changed while the graph stayed fixed. CAST-1 was 1.38 times faster than CAST-2 over four right-hand sides. Over 250, CAST-2 was 1.17 times faster than CAST-1. The crossover lay between ten and fifty solves. The authors could examine the elimination profile after constructing a first factor. Can a cheaper observation predict enough downstream fill to choose well before committing to that expense? [48]

What the smoother leaves for the coarse space

For a symmetric positive-definite matrix A, let the columns of a full-column-rank matrix P describe coarse corrections. The standard coarse matrix defines the exact coarse correction below.

A_c = P^T A P,     x_new = x + P A_c^(-1) P^T (b - A x).

For the error e = x_exact − x, the exact coarse correction removes every vector in the column space of P and leaves every vector with P^T A v = 0 untouched. Its error-propagation operator is

e_new = E_c e,     E_c = I - P (P^T A P)^(-1) P^T A.

Return to the four-cycle 1-2-3-4-1. Let A = L + ε I, with 0 < ε < 1. Give edges 12 and 34 weight one and the other edges weight ε. The two columns of P indicate the pairs {1,2} and {3,4}. Exchange the strong and weak edges to obtain A′. A relabeling takes A to A′, so the spectrum, diagonal and sparsity pattern all agree. The diagram follows the same error v = (1,−1,−1,1)^T through that exchange.

Figure: The shaded regions retain the original aggregates {1,2} and {3,4}. Thick edges have weight 1 and thin edges weight epsilon. After the weights are exchanged, the missed vector has constant sign on the new strong pairs {1,4} and {2,3}, but opposite signs within each retained aggregate. The diagonal shift epsilon I is present in both operators.

A' v = 3 epsilon v,     P^T A' v = 0.

The old pairs see zero coarse residual on both graphs. What changes is the work the coarse level must do. Before the exchange, Av = (2 + ε)v. Afterward, A′v = 3εv. The common diagonal is (1 + 2ε)I, so a weighted Jacobi step on a mode of eigenvalue λ multiplies it by 1 − ωλ/(1 + 2ε). With ω = 2/3 and small ε, the original multiplier approaches −1/3 and the new one approaches 1. An error that relaxation used to shrink now survives almost intact.

Grouping {1,4} and {2,3} puts v in the new coarse space. One exact coarse correction then removes it. On an unfamiliar graph we need an affordable way of discovering [which distinction the retained description misses](https://refactor.press/essays/when-a-description-becomes-a-verdict#enough-for-which-question). Adaptive smoothed aggregation, compatible relaxation and hierarchy-reuse methods already generate or test coarse directions. A proposed diagnostic must improve on those policies after paying for its own observations. An exact two-level calculation gives the target property. Its recursive realization needs a separate analysis. [Appendix B](#solver-evidence-and-extensions) gives examples of how coarse directions are constructed and tested in other matrix classes. [20, 54, 76, 78, 79]

Which promises survive a change?

Some changes admit an inexpensive certificate. Let A = L(w) + μ I and A' = L(w') + μ I on the same graph, with positive edge weights and fixed μ > 0. Choose 0 < c <= 1 <= C so that every edge's new weight divided by its old weight lies between c and C. Comparing the edge energies and the common grounding term gives the first inequality below. For a fixed symmetric positive-definite preconditioner M with an already proved comparison alpha M <= A <= beta M, transitivity gives the second.

c A <= A' <= C A,     c alpha M <= A' <= C beta M.

One exact-arithmetic pass through the weight ratios gives a condition-number bound Cβ/(cα) for the fixed M on the new system. A small group of extreme changes can make those global constants pessimistic. Updating level matrices or smoothers changes M and requires fresh analysis. A floating-point certificate also needs rounding control. Section 9 compares keeping the old hierarchy intact with updating its matrices or rebuilding it. [19, 52]

A small calculation can also locate a missing assumption in a proposed guarantee. APLICUR updates a low-rank preconditioner during an LSQR solve. In the authors’ Theorem 4.1 take the matrix and its rank-one CUR approximation both equal to diag(1,0), regularization μ = 1 and target t = 1/2. The preconditioned augmented matrix has singular values 1/2 and 1. The printed lower bound is 1, and its condition-number bound is √5/2. The actual condition number is 2. The approximation error here is zero. [87]

The point to repair is the retained block of the ideal Gram matrix. Its eigenvalue is t², while the omitted directions have eigenvalue μ². If μ ≤ t ≤ √(σ̂ℓ² + μ²), with σ̂ℓ the smallest retained singular value, and the CUR error E satisfies ||E||₂ < μ, the preconditioned singular values lie between μ − ||E||₂ and t + ||E||₂. The regularized default satisfies the added target restriction. Each phase still needs the approximation-error condition. Note 87 gives the calculation from the paper’s preconditioner. [87]

What would count as a discovery here? Show which interactions or error directions the representation preserves. Find a test that distinguishes a missing mode from a bad smoother or a loss of precision. Explain why the repair addresses that failure. Then take it to an unfamiliar instance and charge for the probes, rejected fixes and fallback. Whoever can do that has improved the solver. Who will do it first?


7. Agents already take part in this programme

Agents already inspect solver code, measure errors and invent tests. A specialist who claims an advantage has to beat an agent free to do all three.

LinearSolveBench released sparse solvers selected from 640 candidates per model on 96 magnetic-diffusion cases. The best passed the specified checks on all 96 and ran about 1.85 times faster than GMRES(50) with BoomerAMG when the original case times were summed. The timed work covered solver creation, setup and solution. Discovery, compilation and independent verification were outside that clock. The release adds a fresh correctness check on the same cases. Speed on a new family is an open question. [32]

In SVG-EAR, Astra wrote a probe that periodically compared sparse attention with exact attention on a small sample of queries. Its code assigned a budget to each head using the measured error and fell back to dense attention when necessary. Astra also supplied a variance correction. The final code reduced mean runtime by about 18 percent, improved all three mean fidelity measures and passed 43 of 48 hidden checks. The study assessed the combined changes. The five fidelity failures also occurred in a separate exact-attention implementation. An agent had devised an informative measurement and used it to change the calculation. [70]

What happens to a human contribution once it can be written down? In a finite-element task in SciCode-Verified, agents kept replacing the specified weak form with its textbook counterpart. Restating the equations at implementation time corrected that choice. Across 64 corrected main problems, expert background raised GPT-5.5 from 65.6 to 90.6 percent, Gemini 3.5 Flash from 51.6 to 87.5 percent, and Claude Opus 4.8 from 57.8 to 78.1 percent. Each comparison used a single run and counted a pass in either test environment. Writing the background and independently rediscovering it were both unpriced. Once every team receives it, a later gain from using it cannot be credited again to a specialist’s presence. [35]

The autonomous competitor can also change how it searches. AIDE2 reports seven accepted changes to its own research harness during an eight-day autonomous run, with gains on four held-out benchmarks. Its comparator was a human-engineered production agent. Whoever finds the next solver, and however they search for it, we need a way to decide whether the answer it returns is accurate enough. [55]


8. Guarantees at the cost of the complete calculation

How much error can the computed answer still contain?

The four-cycle showed an error that vanished from the coarse residual. We can test the answer against the original system. For symmetric positive-definite A and M, suppose we have proved αM ≤ A with α > 0. Given an approximation y to Ax = b, compute its residual r = b − Ay. Reversing the matrix inequality under inversion gives

||x - y||_A^2 = r^T A^(-1) r <= (1/alpha) r^T M^(-1) r.

This bounds the energy error. When solving with M is cheap and α is large enough to give a useful bound, the certificate is worth computing. After a weight change, use cα from Section 6 and recompute r against A′. For singular Laplacians, restrict the comparison to the orthogonal complement of the common kernel. An estimated spectral comparison, an approximate solve with M, and rounding in the residual and quadratic form each require control before the calculated number can certify the answer. [21, 52]

The shifted-Laplacian pilot also admits a conservative check independent of the preconditioner. Since A' >= μ I, any validated upper bound ρ on the Euclidean norm of r = b - A' y gives

||x - y||_A' <= ||r||_2 / sqrt(mu) <= rho / sqrt(mu).

For the shifted graph, a validated norm bound ρ thus certifies the absolute energy tolerance τ whenever ρ/√μ ≤ τ. This check works even when the quality of the updated hierarchy is unknown. It tells us whether the answer is good enough. Its conservatism can determine how long we keep iterating. [21, 52]

For a nonzero residual, call the ratio of the exact-residual bound to the true energy error R. On an eigenmode with eigenvalue λ, R = √(λ/μ). For the four-cycle’s missed mode this is exactly √3, at any contrast. Higher-energy modes can produce much larger overestimates. Rounding control adds a separate factor ρ/||r||₂. On known solutions we can measure the iterations each margin costs.

R^2 = (r^T r)/(mu r^T A'^(-1) r),     1 <= R <= sqrt(lambda_max(A')/mu).

When the arithmetic changes the verdict

Which residual did the computer measure? Pipelined conjugate gradients overlaps global communication with useful work. Its extra recurrences preserve the standard algorithm in exact arithmetic but can amplify rounding error. The stored residual can then look small while b − Ay for the returned iterate is still large. Cools and collaborators derived an estimate that triggers selective recomputation of the residual and auxiliary quantities. Their experiments recovered attainable accuracy while retaining efficient parallel performance. A replacement costs matrix-vector products and may delay convergence. Its timing is already the subject of an automatic rule. [95]

Even an accurately computed discrepancy can miss part of the requested answer. The FEniCSx benchmark’s joint NaN mask excludes a location when either the reference or prediction is NaN. Some reference points legitimately lie outside the domain. A missing prediction at a required point poses a different problem. Fix the required domain from the supplied problem, check that predictions there are finite, and report coverage beside the error. [33]

What the accepted answer cost

Saving a quarter of the iterations can buy almost nothing in wall time. On 1,000 AirfRANS systems the selected learned smoother of Chillón and collaborators converged on 99.9 percent. It used about 27 percent fewer V-cycles than relaxed Jacobi but saved only about 4 percent of solve time. The network predicted coefficients during setup. Applying the resulting sparse smoother cost more per cycle than diagonal Jacobi. On separate fine three-dimensional canonical-flow tests, convergence averaged 67.7 percent across ten trained models. The tolerance was 10^-4 and the cap was 500 cycles. Both the workload and the way a model was selected belong beside its performance figure. [40]

Shui and colleagues found lower mean latency for selected human-assisted GPU kernels than for autonomous artifacts on five matched B200 operator definitions, by factors of 1.35 to 13.25. The fastest top-k indexer passed a three-trial check and then failed three of 200 targeted trials. An earlier fallback passed 1,206 targeted trials. The other four definitions retained reported latency advantages. Different model choices, development budgets and supplied context leave the contribution of human guidance unresolved. [102]

For N comparable queries, include setup and the cost of diagnosing, solving and checking each one. Recovery includes the further attempts and fallback allowed by the declared timeout rules. Report remaining failures as well as expected time.

T(N) = T_setup + N (T_diagnose + T_solve + T_check + E[T_recovery]).

With extra setup cost ΔS and net saving d > 0 per query, expected break-even begins at N ≥ ΔS/d. Costs after a coefficient change can be correlated, so the mean needs an accompanying distribution. A diagnostic that takes several solves to evaluate can exhaust a four-solve job. A rule discovered once and reused on thousands of such jobs can still repay a long investigation. Each deployment pays for its own probes, checks and recovery. The research comparison in Section 9 separately charges all teams for finding and validating their rules over the same horizon. [48, 62, 86]


9. Test the method, then the specialist contribution

Four right-hand sides remain to be solved on a grid whose coefficients have just changed. The old hierarchy is already paid for. A full rebuild may prevent a slow solve. A cheap probe may tell us we can keep what we have. Can someone invent a rule that makes the decision in time to save work? And if a team invents one, what evidence would show that having a specialist on the team helped?

What would establish each claim?
Claim | Required evidence
Better performance on a stated workload | Independent measurements at the same accuracy, including setup, checking, and recovery.
Transfer beyond development cases | Withheld families with meaningful changes in geometry, coefficients, topology, or other relevant structure.
A uniform mathematical guarantee | A valid argument covering the stated class, with its assumptions and parameter dependence explicit.

Two projects worth comparing

Start the solver pilot with a shifted grid Laplacian A(w) = L(w) + μ I, positive edge weights and fixed μ > 0. Build an initial multigrid hierarchy, change the weights once, and solve the changed system A′x = b. Every trial starts with the same initial hierarchy for its operator. A selector has three choices. [76, 78, 79]

Three responses to the same change in edge weights.
Policy | Operation after a weight change
F (fixed) | Retain the old preconditioner in full, including every level matrix, smoother, transfer and coarse factorization.
U (updated) | Retain the transfers. Recompute the Galerkin operators, smoothers and coarse factorization.
R (rebuilt) | Build a new hierarchy with the original setup procedure.

Only F keeps the preconditioner covered by the weight-ratio argument in Section 6. Each policy can have its answers checked by the independent energy-error bound in Section 8. Compare the selector with each fixed policy and with an existing adaptive policy, at the same accepted accuracy on unseen changes. Count the probe, the failed attempt and any rebuild. A rule that spends five solves finding the right policy for a four-solve job has answered the mathematical question too late.

Give the structural team an equally serious problem. Huang and collaborators obtained a rational polynomial of degree 23 whose splitting field has Galois group M₂₃. Forty-digit expansions and a change of scale led to exact equations for one cover. For related covers, even sixty digits left some coefficients unrecognizable. The team used all six other permutation triples, combined conjugate data, and chose a new scale from the coefficients it could already recognize. Algebraic verification completed the argument. The authors describe AI-assisted computation under human direction. [110]

A prospective project could give each team previously withheld branch data and a proposed base field, then ask whether a cover realizing those data can be recovered and certified. An affirmative result would give exact equations and certify the relevant branching and monodromy. A negative result would prove an obstruction of the scope named in the brief. The team chooses its coordinates, precision, theory and route to verification. In a full sparse project, give the team the changing operators, workload and accuracy requirement and the same freedom to invent a representation or prove a limitation. Restricting choices to F, U and R belongs to the small method pilot. The full projects start before anyone has identified the missing lemma or chosen a solver.

Decide in advance what a useful obstruction earns. Suppose a team proves that a specified family of cheap observations gives identical answers on two allowed changes that require different actions to meet the cost and accuracy target. That can settle whether the proposed policy class will suffice. In a cover project, a proof excluding the prescribed class of realizations earns the corresponding credit if its scope settles the declared question. A stalled search earns neither verdict. Flag in advance which briefs admit a positive construction, a decisive obstruction, or both. Report the two kinds of result separately as well as success under each brief. In the four-solve performance pilot the positive outcome still requires a faster accepted method. Evidence for stopping a search and any demonstrable work it saves get their own record.

What the specialist changes

Run the same equipped agent alone, with a specialist, and with a mathematically capable generalist. All may change the representation, improve the search or challenge a faulty checker. Give them the same starting evidence and charge each group for the work that follows, including advice that leads nowhere. Let C_A and C_H be the total research cost when the agent alone and the specialist-agent team first produce an independently accepted result for that brief. A run with no accepted result within its protocol has infinite cost for this comparison. At a fixed research budget b the specialist premium is

Delta(b) = Pr(C_H <= b) - Pr(C_A <= b).

Measure the gain over the generalist-assisted team too. If the claim is that collaboration outperforms either partner alone, add specialists working with the same software and sources without generative AI. In the algebra experiment with Archon, a supervisor suggested a Krull-domain proof. Archon kept its own Kaplansky argument, took the intermediate observations it needed, and finished in 2 hours 12 minutes. Left alone at the same checkpoint, it finished in 3 hours 43 minutes. The preparation time for the blueprint was uncharged. Counting it is part of finding out what the intervention bought. [90, 103, 104]

The test most likely to change my mind comes one round later. Give every team the successful rules, counterexamples and proofs. On new problems measure what the archive saves, including the cost of finding and using its contents. Then ask what the specialist adds with those discoveries already in everyone’s hands. Berlot-Attwell and collaborators found negligible direct reuse across problems for LEGO-Prover’s learned lemmas and evidence against its proposed indirect reuse mechanism. An entry in a library has earned its place when someone can use it. [89]

Assign mixed projects before their outcomes are known, by the deliverable in the original brief. The arithmetic cover belongs with structural work even if finding it takes most of the machine time. A solver project stays with sparse computation even if its decisive step is a theorem. If a brief genuinely asks for both deliverables, designate it as mixed in advance and report it separately. Within each portfolio keep the natural frequency of ambiguous, expensive decisions. A second comparison matches projects on those features and asks what happens at similar difficulty.

At the same budget, D(b) is the sparse specialist premium minus the structural specialist premium. The claim needs a worthwhile sparse gain over autonomy, a worthwhile gain beyond a capable generalist, and evidence that the corresponding gain is larger in the selected sparse portfolio. If advice hurts both teams and hurts the algebra team more, D(b) is positive and I have lost my case for employing a numerical specialist. The [experimental protocol](#experimental-protocol) gives the decision margins and uncertainty rules. These comparisons price the help of trained people on specified projects. The scientific value of their discoveries and the cost of training their successors require their own account.


10. Where to concentrate research effort

I would put a specialist where the choice of what to retain, measure or bound can change the theorem and a wrong choice is expensive to pursue. The work may be to see an error both old aggregates miss, to find coordinates that expose a candidate, or to find other coordinates that certify it. An agent that proves its own search family inadequate is doing that work too. The case for employing the specialist depends on what happens with that agent available. [106, 107]

I would put the first research dollar into adaptive sparse computation. The problem keeps changing after a useful method exists. Coefficients change the difficult modes. Elimination changes the cost of the next elimination. Precision changes whether a recurrence delivers the accuracy it promises. The consequences can be measured, and many solves can repay one good diagnosis. Overdetermined elliptic eigenfunctions and exact reconstruction of arithmetic covers deserve serious comparison. There, too, a change of variables can turn a plausible object into one a team can prove exists. [48, 86, 95, 97, 98, 107, 110, 111]

Give everyone each successful discovery, then run the next round. My bet is that specialists produce a worthwhile gain in the chance of an accepted discovery after their time is charged, that they beat capable generalists, and that the gain is larger on the selected sparse projects. If the comparison rules out a worthwhile specialist gain, retire the claim for those projects. If it reliably favors the structural teams, change the ranking. I would make the first allocation now and let the results decide the next one.

A mathematician can invent a method so good that nobody needs its inventor on the next hundred cases. That is a mathematical success. When the hundred-and-first case breaks the method, who finds the missing distinction, and what does it cost?


